2024-09-05 16:22:52,344 - app - INFO - Analysis result: {'overall_feedback': "Olusgun, my friend, this resume reads like a LinkedIn post written by a chatbot that had a little too much caffeine. It's long, it's detailed, but it's also a bit...well, let's just say it needs a good editor.  You've got the skills and experience, but you're drowning them in a sea of words. Think of it this way: your resume is like a Tinder profile. You want to capture attention, not bore people with a novel.", 'feedback_cards': [{'title': 'Contact Information', 'description': 'Olusgun, you\'ve got all the right information, but this section is screaming for a makeover.  That \'Greater Accra Region\' is giving me serious "I live in a suburb, but I\'m trying to be cool" vibes.  And  \'bishoptewogbade@gmail.com\'  -  don\'t get me wrong, it\'s classic, but it\'s time to upgrade to a professional email address.  Think of it as a LinkedIn profile for your inbox.', 'recommendations': 'Lose the "Greater Accra Region."  It\'s like saying you\'re from "the Earth."  Keep it simple.  Get a LinkedIn profile,  and use a more professional email address - something like  \'olusegun.ogbade@domain.com.\'  You know, something that\'ll make you look like the pro you are.'}, {'title': 'Summary', 'description': "Olusgun, this summary is longer than a Shakespearean sonnet.  It's a bit of a word vomit. You've got the skills, but you're trying to impress with a thesaurus. Just remember,  'impactful' and 'dedicated' are buzzwords that are begging to be replaced with something a bit more unique. You're a talented developer, but you don't need to shout it from the rooftops.", 'recommendations': 'Keep it concise.  Target your summary to the specific job you\'re applying for.  Avoid buzzwords -  they\'re like the cheese on a pizza, too much and it\'s overwhelming.  Focus on what makes you unique and memorable.  Think: "If I was hiring, what would make me want to call Olusgun for an interview?"'}, {'title': 'Education', 'description': "Olusgun, I appreciate the bold font for  'BSC COMPUTER SCIENCE.'  It's a solid degree, no doubt.  But let's face it, this section is as exciting as watching paint dry. You've got all the important information - what you studied and where you studied it - but it's a bit...plain.  It's like a beige sweater - functional but not exactly exciting.  Think about how you can make it pop.", 'recommendations': "Add a relevant course or project you're particularly proud of.  Maybe mention a GPA that screams 'I was the smartest person in the class.'  Give the reader a little something to remember about your academic journey."}, {'title': 'Skills', 'description': "Olusgun, this is a classic case of 'throwing everything at the wall and seeing what sticks.' You've got the right skills - Java, Python, Flutter, all the essentials - but you're not making a strong case for why these skills are valuable.  It's like going to a buffet and taking a little bit of everything, but leaving with a plate of food that doesn't really taste good. ", 'recommendations': "Focus on the skills that are most relevant to the job you're applying for.   Group the skills into categories (like programming languages, frameworks, etc.)  This makes it easier for recruiters to find what they're looking for.  And think about how you can showcase your skills through examples or quantifiable achievements."}]}
