2024-09-05 16:24:38,427 - app - INFO - Analysis result: {'overall_feedback': "Olusgun, my friend, this resume is like a delicious bowl of fufu - it's got all the right ingredients, but the presentation needs some serious work. It's packed with information, but it's so dense it could make a librarian lose their glasses.  You're clearly a talented developer with a knack for tech, but this resume makes you sound more like a tech encyclopedia than a human being.  Let's loosen up a bit, eh?", 'feedback_cards': [{'title': 'Contact Information', 'description': "Olusgun, this section is like a Nigerian street vendor - you've got all the contact info you need, but it's all crammed together in a chaotic mess.  It's a bit overwhelming for a recruiter trying to reach you.  Maybe consider putting some space between your phone number, email address, and LinkedIn profile link - it'll make you look less desperate and more organized.  Also, 'Greater Accra Region' -  come on, dude, at least say a specific location. Are you hoping to be recruited by a mysterious tech entity that only exists in the ether of Accra?  Give us something to work with!"}, {'title': 'Summary', 'description': "Your Summary is like a tech conference keynote - ambitious, packed with buzzwords, but a little too long.  We get it, you're passionate about fintech, AI, and mobile app development.  But let's cut down the technical jargon and use some language that's a bit more human.  Imagine you're talking to a friend about your work, what would you say?  Don't worry, you can still impress them with your technical skills, just make sure they understand it without having to consult a dictionary.  "}, {'title': 'Experience', 'description': "Olusgun, this section is like your code - clean, organized, and efficient.  You've listed all your previous roles in detail, and you've even included some impressive numbers to back up your claims.  But there's a slight problem: you've fallen into the trap of bullet point purgatory.  Each bullet point is a mini-paragraph, crammed with so much information it's hard to absorb.  Don't be afraid to break up those long sentences, use more descriptive language, and add a little personality.  Remember, you're not writing a technical document, you're telling a story about your skills and achievements.  Make it engaging, my friend, make it engaging!"}, {'title': 'Education', 'description': "Olusgun, your education section is as simple as a well-written line of code - clean, concise, and to the point.  A solid foundation, nothing too fancy, but it does the job.  However, it's a bit lonely.  Why not add a line or two about a particularly interesting project or a relevant skill you learned during your studies? It's a great opportunity to showcase your creativity and passion for computer science."}, {'title': 'Skills', 'description': "Your skills list is like a buffet table at a Nigerian wedding -  there's so much variety, it's hard to know where to start.  You've got all the right ingredients: Java, Python, Dart, SQL, Flutter, Flask...  But, like a buffet table, it could use a little organization.  Group your skills into relevant categories (programming languages, frameworks, tools, etc.).  It'll make it easier for a recruiter to see at a glance what you've got to offer.  And, remember,  sometimes less is more.  Pick the skills that are most relevant to the job you're applying for and showcase those prominently."}]}
